  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "Consider the following initial value problem\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{-y},\\,y(1)=1.\n$$\n\nYou're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(A+x)$ is the solution to this initial value problem. What is the value of the constant $A?$",
  "worked_solution": "We're given that $y=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(A+x)$ is the solution to the initial value problem. Therefore, it must satisfy the differential equation *and* the initial condition $y(1)=1.$\n\nSubstituting the initial condition $y(1)=1$ into our solution and solving for $A,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(A+x) \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(A+1) \\\\ {e}^{1} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(A+1)} \\\\ e & =A+1 \\\\ A & =e-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(e-1+x).\n$$",
